const faq = [
  { q: 'Where does the Thick-billed Raven live?', a: 'The Thick-billed Raven (Corvus crassirostris) is essentially endemic to Ethiopia, with range extensions into adjacent Eritrea, Somalia (northern highlands), and Djibouti. It is confined almost entirely to the Ethiopian Highlands — the high plateaus, gorges, and escarpments above approximately 1,500 m elevation. It is the dominant large corvid in its range and is essentially not found outside Ethiopia and Eritrea.' },
  { q: 'Is the Thick-billed Raven the largest raven in the world?', a: 'The Thick-billed Raven is the largest corvid in Africa and one of the largest corvids in the world by body mass. Adult birds weigh 1,000–1,500 g, with the largest individuals comparable to the largest Common Ravens. By wing length and total bulk, Thick-billed Ravens are substantial birds — the extreme bill alone adds to the impression of size. The Common Raven (Corvus corax) is similar in mass, and the two species are approximately equal in size overall.' },
  { q: 'Can you see Thick-billed Ravens as a tourist in Ethiopia?', a: 'Yes. The Thick-billed Raven is a conspicuous bird in Addis Ababa and throughout the Ethiopian Highlands — it is not shy and is regularly encountered in towns, around markets, on building rooftops, and on cliffs in the highlands. The Simien Mountains National Park (a UNESCO World Heritage Site) offers particularly good views, with ravens visible soaring over the Simien escarpment. They are also common at highland towns like Lalibela, Gondar, and Bahir Dar.' },
];
